    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p8_b29",
      "text": "Results and Analysis. The results of our human evalua-\ntion, presented in Figure 6, provide clear evidence of our\nmethods\u2019 success. Both LBR and LBP are shown to effec-\ntively mitigate multiple types of hallucinations compared to\nthe baseline. This outcome validates our core motivation:\nthat explicitly targeting and reducing language bias is a\npotent strategy for enhancing the trustworthiness of LVLMs.\nFurthermore, we observe an interesting trade-off with the\nvanilla DPO model (relative to the VIT baseline). While it\nsignificantly reduces Existence-type hallucinations, it con-\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.19,
        599.74,
        291.19,
        717.49
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        2398,
        1164,
        2869
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 583,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p8_b30",
      "text": "currently increases hallucinations across nearly all other\ncategories. This phenomenon serves as further evidence of\nlanguage bias acquired during DPO training. It suggests the\nmodel is learning to mimic linguistic patterns in the prefer-\nence data, rather than achieving a deeper, visually grounded\nunderstanding of the content.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.44,
        331.34,
        543.1,
        401.15
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1229,
        1325,
        2172,
        1604
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 329,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p8_b31",
      "text": "6.3. Case Study\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.44,
        416.23,
        372.75,
        426.19
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1229,
        1664,
        1491,
        1704
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 15,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p8_b32",
      "text": "We present a representative case study in Figure 5 to quali-\ntatively illustrate the benefits of LBP. The baseline model\nproduces a description with several factual inaccuracies. It\nerroneously claims there are two cars, describes the position\nof a non-existent vehicle, and hallucinates a fire hydrant\non the left. According to our evaluation protocol, these er-\nrors constitute one Number and one Existence hallucination.\nIn contrast, the description generated by our LBP-aligned\nmodel is factually accurate and entirely free of such errors.\nThis example vividly demonstrates LBP\u2019s effectiveness in\nsuppressing object hallucinations and producing more trust-\nworthy, visually grounded responses. Additional case stud-\nies of LBR and LBP are provided in Section E.\n",
      "bbox": [
        306.97,
        434.96,
        543.19,
        588.4
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1227,
        1739,
        2172,
        2353
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.99,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 765,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p8_b33",
